course: dictionary
lesson: 12264
title: Contempt: looking down on someone
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Contempt is a strong feeling that someone or something is worthless and does not deserve any respect. It is stronger than dislike, because you do not just dislike a person, you think they are beneath you. Writers often show contempt without naming it, through a sneer, a cold voice or turning away. «Mia gave Tom a look of contempt and walked off without a word.»
explain: Read the clues around the word. «He spoke to the new boy with contempt.» The words with contempt tell you how he spoke, and the boy is being looked down on. To check your meaning, swap in a word like scorn or disrespect. If the sentence still makes sense, you have got it.
explain: Contempt also appears in set phrases. «Familiarity breeds contempt» is a saying: if you know something too well, you can stop respecting it. Something «beneath contempt» is so bad that it does not even deserve contempt. Learn these phrases as whole chunks, not just one word at a time.
fact: Contempt means the feeling that someone or something is worthless and does not deserve respect.
fact: «Contempt» is an uncountable noun, so you say «a feeling of contempt», not «a contempt».
fact: «She held him in contempt after he cheated.» means she thought he was not worth respecting.
fact: «Familiarity breeds contempt» means knowing something too well can make you stop respecting it.
fact: Contempt of court is a crime: it means disobeying a court or being rude to a judge.
exercise: «The king looked at the messenger with contempt.» What does this show about the king? | He was afraid of the dark | He was feeling sleepy and bored | He was delighted to see the messenger | *He thought the messenger was worthless
exercise: Which of these is a sign of contempt in a story? | A loud laugh of joy | *A sneer and a turn away | A tight hug of welcome | A warm hug
exercise: In «Familiarity breeds contempt», what does contempt mean? | A cold drink on a hot day | Being too busy to talk | *Lack of respect that can grow from knowing something too well | Great love and admiration
test: «The villain spoke to the guards with cold contempt.» What does cold contempt tell us? | *The villain looked down on the guards | The villain was lost in the castle | The villain was tired and sleepy | The villain was kind and gentle
test: «Her cruel words were beneath contempt.» What does beneath contempt mean? | Very expensive and rare | Kindly and gently spoken | *So bad they do not even deserve contempt | Hidden under the table
exam: Which is true about contempt of court? | It means being very polite to a judge | It is a prize given to good readers | It is a kind of hat worn in court | *It is a crime to disobey a court or be rude to a judge
